    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186096841,
      "id": "29956ecb-a28a-5406-b8dc-9776e9a3ccee",
      "title": "Differentiating a Polynomial Raised to a Power Using the Chain Rule"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7a341ecd-c3d9-5c0e-bdc5-58565219404d",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}$ when $f(x)=(-7+6x)^{5}.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as a composite function:\n\n$$\nf=g^{5},\\,g=-7+6x\n$$\n\nThe chain rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}g}=5g^{4},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}=6.\n$$\n\nFinally, multiplying the two together and substituting for $g$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} &=5\\cdot 6\\cdot (-7+6x)^{4} \\\\  &=30(-7+6x)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "fc7ffdec-be04-5a06-8cea-2fe524306882",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/radio",
